From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:01:36 +0000
Subject: Restrict executable memory in JIT-less renderers

A renderer for a site with JIT blocked runs V8 with --jitless. V8 then
runs bytecode in its interpreter and its builtins from the loaded
library, and keeps its code pages without execute permission, so the
renderer has no use for new executable memory.

Add two flags for Android. The report flag is off by default. The block
flag is on by default on Android 17 and later, where renderers on the
primary ABI run without the Java runtime, and off by default on earlier
versions:

"Block executable memory in renderers without JIT" makes the seccomp
filter of such a renderer end the renderer when it asks for new
executable memory through mmap, mmap2, mprotect or pkey_mprotect. Memory
that is already mapped both writable and executable stays usable, which
includes the code cache of the Java runtime in renderers on Android
versions before 17, and the rule does not cover writes through
/proc/self/mem.

"Report executable memory in renderers without JIT" logs these requests
and lets them succeed, so that test runs can show whether anything in
such a renderer still needs executable memory before the block is
turned on. A renderer in this mode makes one request right after the
filter starts, which shows in the log that reporting works.

The block takes precedence over the report flag. A renderer counts as
JIT-less when --jitless is one of its --js-flags, the same test that
turns on dynamic code prohibition for renderers on Windows. Renderers
for sites with JIT allowed are not affected.

The default of the block flag is read from the API level of the system
when the library loads, so the browser and its renderers compute the same
default. A state chosen on the flags page takes precedence. On Android 17
and later the report flag therefore needs the block flag set to Disabled.

With either flag, the renderer also logs the executable mappings it
already has that are writable, shared or anonymous, such as the code
cache of the Java runtime. The filter cannot take those away.

